  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms return to the area \nduring the second half of the weekend and potentially lingering\ninto early next week.\n\nEnsemble guidance advances a strong upper level trough from the\nGreat Lakes into the Ohio River Valley on Sunday and into \nMonday. This cut off low pressure system and associated cold \nfront brings increasing chances for showers and a few t-storms \nin our area through Monday. Shear and instability values seem \nlow due to weak winds aloft and widespread cloudiness, so severe\nthreat appears very low. Rainfall amounts of half to one inch \nspaced over a long period of time mean this rainfall would be \nlargely beneficial and should not cause any flooding concerns. \n\nContinuing into Tuesday and Wednesday, this cold front may to stall \nout to the the south of our area. Thus, showers and a few t-storms \ncould linger into the later half of next week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Primarily dry conditions through Saturday.\n\nUpper level low pressure is spinning over southern New England\ntoday, while a surface low moves off Cape Cod. The gradient\nbetween this low and high pressure in the Great Lakes/Ohio\nValley is resulting in another day of gusty northwest winds.\nWater vapor imagery is showing deeper moisture with the upper \nlow is pulling to the east. Even so, some models still show \nanother spoke of energy rotating southward this afternoon and \nevening, perhaps producing a few showers across northeastern \nMaryland. The current forecast is dry, and am not convinced \nthere is a high enough chance of rain to add it to the forecast.\nRH forecasts do suggest cumulus or altocumulus clouds may \nincrease across the northeastern quadrant of the CWA this \nafternoon. Temperatures will remain seasonable or slightly below\nnormal through tonight, when lighter winds may allow rural \nareas to decouple. Highs this afternoon are generally in the \n80s, with lows tonight from the mid 50s to mid 60s.\n\nThe surface high will reach the area Friday. Shortwave ridging\nwill approach from the west as the upper low departs New England.\nDry conditions are expected with high temperatures closer to \nclimatology in the mid 80s to near 90 and lows in the 60s to \naround 70.\n\nTemperatures may warm a couple more degrees Saturday as the high\nmoves off the coast and increasing southerly flow develops.\nClouds will likely start increasing as low pressure at the\nsurface and aloft moves into the Ohio Valley. There is some\nuncertainty with how quickly rain chances arrive. Some showers\ncould reach the Alleghenies as early as Saturday afternoon, but\nwill become increasingly probable Saturday night. Much of the\narea, especially east of the Blue Ridge, may remain dry until\nSunday.",
